{
  "note": "The look of the open world's ground (ui/play/ground_art.gd draws it, ui/play/tile_view.gd uses it, tools/check_data.gd checks it). Each ground kind (a biome, water, deep water, snow, ford) has `variants`: one weight for each look it can take, 2 to 4, and a tile takes one by a hash of its place, never a die, so a given tile always looks the same and its neighbours differ. A variant is the kind's flat colour (tiles.colors in data/world/sandbox.json) with its `patterns` scattered over it, each pattern a `type` (speck, tuft, pebble, streak, crack, flake), a `count` of them, and either a `shift` (lighter above 0, darker below, of the base colour) or a `color`. Water has `glints` too: a shimmer of light dashes that sway across `frames` frames (`sway` is how far each is moved in each frame, `pulse` how bright it is), played at `water_fps` frames a second, and still (the first frame) with reduced motion. `edges` makes the soft joins: where two land kinds meet, the kind with the higher `rank` bleeds a ragged, fading band onto the other (`profile` is how far in it reaches in each pixel of the side, `alphas` how solid each pixel row is); where water meets land the water tile gets a light foam edge (`foam`). Nothing here is random and nothing changes the world: it is only how it is drawn.",
